# Live system
live-boot
live-config
live-config-systemd
cryptsetup
cryptsetup-initramfs
sudo
user-setup

# Desktop (light: 8 GB machines)
xfce4
xfce4-terminal
# The X server and the login screen: only "recommended" by xfce4/lightdm, and recommends
# are off, so they must be named or LightDM fails at boot. xserver-xorg brings every
# video and input driver (xserver-xorg-video-all, -input-all).
xserver-xorg
xserver-xorg-core
lightdm
lightdm-gtk-greeter
firefox-esr
network-manager
network-manager-gnome
# Wi-Fi: network-manager only recommends these, and auto/config turns recommends off.
# Without wpasupplicant NetworkManager cannot use Wi-Fi at all.
wpasupplicant
wireless-regdb
iw
# Sticks and Windows drives open by themselves when plugged in (Thunar), exFAT and NTFS
# included; screen and power handled (without it the screen blanks after 10 minutes).
thunar-volman
gvfs-backends
exfatprogs
ntfs-3g
xfce4-power-manager
# Compressed swap in memory instead of on disk (/etc/systemd/zram-generator.conf).
systemd-zram-generator
# Sound, Bluetooth, cooling, video (named because recommends are off; checked against the
# owner's Dell XPS 13 9380): a sound server for Jarvis's voice and the browser, Bluetooth
# for speakers and controllers, Intel's thermal daemon against overheating and throttling,
# and Intel video decoding for playback and ffmpeg.
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
pipewire-alsa
wireplumber
alsa-utils
# Only Recommends/Suggests in Debian 13.7, so recommends-off never installed them (checked
# 2026-10-20 with apt in a clean trixie; packages.debian.org/trixie/alsa-ucm-conf): the
# sound routing for laptop chips like the XPS's ("absolutely necessary to make some sound
# hardware work"), and PipeWire's Bluetooth audio (headphones and speakers).
alsa-ucm-conf
alsa-topology-conf
# PipeWire never started on the stick (VM with an Intel HD Audio card, 2026-10-28): its user
# service "Requires=pipewire.socket dbus.service", and the user dbus.service comes only with
# dbus-user-session, which pipewire-bin merely Recommends (with rtkit, which gives the sound
# real-time priority so it does not crackle while the model runs). Installed by hand in that
# VM, PipeWire started and listed "Built-in Audio Analog Stereo".
dbus-user-session
rtkit
# Jarvis's voice without an ElevenLabs key is Firefox's own (speechSynthesis), which needs
# Speech Dispatcher (support.mozilla.org "Speech Dispatcher required for SpeechSynthesis
# API"). On the desktop, 2026-10-28, Debian 13: Jarvis silent; Speech Dispatcher 0.12.0-5
# with espeak-ng gave only the "dummy" voice (spd-say -L); the espeak-ng voices came only
# with its espeak-ng output module (0.12.0-5, the package below).
speech-dispatcher
speech-dispatcher-espeak-ng
libspa-0.2-bluetooth
pavucontrol
bluez
blueman
thermald
intel-media-va-driver
fonts-dejavu
dbus-x11

# BAU control plane and its host protections
python3
python3-venv
python3-yaml
nftables
apparmor
apparmor-utils
chrony
openssh-client
git
curl
ca-certificates
openssl

# Everything BAU's install.sh needs, so a laptop installed from this stick is finished
# with no network (install.sh --offline; tests/test_full_stick.py keeps this complete).
podman
uidmap
auditd
unattended-upgrades
bind9-dnsutils
ffmpeg

# Encrypted storage setup (jarvis-storage)
fdisk
util-linux
e2fsprogs

# llama.cpp runtime (built from source by image/build-live.sh)
libgomp1
libssl3t64

# Diagnostics and recovery
pciutils
usbutils
lshw
htop
less
nano

# Firmware for common laptops: Wi-Fi, graphics, sound (explicit: the installer-style
# firmware packages such as firmware-b43-installer download at install time and are left out)
firmware-linux-free
firmware-misc-nonfree
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-realtek
firmware-atheros
firmware-brcm80211
firmware-mediatek
firmware-amd-graphics
firmware-intel-graphics
firmware-intel-sound
firmware-sof-signed
intel-microcode
amd64-microcode

# The desktop's graphics card (docs/DESKTOP.md): llama.cpp's Vulkan part needs the Vulkan
# loader to start; the NVIDIA driver itself comes later, with sudo bau-gpu-setup.
libvulkan1
